  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1245 PM Sunday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... High pressure will allow for a continued period of \ndry weather with near or slightly below temperatures this week. \n\nEarly afternoon satellite imagery and surface obs show a 1038mb \nsurface ridge over western Pennsylvania, with ridging extending \nsouthward into North Carolina. A deepening area of low pressure \nresides off the coast, located along the stalled cold front that has \nbrought significantly cooler temperatures to much of the East Coast. \nAs the low has deepened, this has tightened the pressure gradient \nacross central NC with most/if not all observing sites reporting \ngusty northeasterly winds around 20kts. \n\nThe overall synoptic setup this week will feature surface and upper \nlevel high pressure in control of the weather across central NC. The \noffshore low will maintain a relatively tight pressure gradient \nacross the area through mid week, yielding afternoon breeziness out \nof the north, but little in the way of other sensible weather \nimpacts. Temps each afternoon will be within a few degrees of normal \nfor mid/late October, with highs in the upper 60s to mid 70s. \nMeanwhile, morning lows should generally fall into the 40s. Frost \nshould not be a concern this week given a few kts of wind lingering \nthrough the overnight hours, although it would not be surprising if \nsome of the normally cooler spots in the western Piedmont saw temps \nreach the upper 30s tonight and again Monday night.",
